I am a classic millennial I'm highly  jaded and highly optimistic at the same  time I'm jaded because let's face it  were the generation that inherited all  the consequences of bad decisions from  previous generations climate change  wealth inequality broken health care and  to me businesses seem to be the biggest  culprits of these problems but I'm also  optimistic that businesses can be a  force for good and given the size of the  private sector businesses need to be  forces for good if we want real scale  change so how do we make businesses do  good of course as a millennial I said  I'm a problem solver I'm gifted and  special I'm going to single-handedly  solve this problem you're welcome guys  after college I went into investing to  understand and influence how businesses  make decisions in my job I came across a  lot of businesses and a lot of bad  decisions here's one well-known example  the BP oil spill in 2010 BP was facing a  delay in their drilling operations every  day of delay cost them 1 million dollars  under immense pressure they rushed a  launch of an oil rig in the Gulf of  Mexico the rig exploded causing the  largest oil spill in history killing 11  people and damaging our planet in ways  we still don't fully know today this was  costly for society but also costly for  BP to save 1 million dollars a day BP  cost its shareholders 100 billion  dollars  I call this nearsightedness focusing so  much on what's in front of you that  you're missing the bigger picture let me  ask you a question who here is near  so you have trouble seing what's far  from you but you can see clearly what's  close I am seriously nearsighted and we  all know how dangerous and frustrating  it is imagine one day you're outside  without your glasses or contacts you're  a disaster waiting to happen you might  be scanning the ground for something you  might trip on and suddenly a pole hits  your face traditional business thinking  is nearsighted focusing so much on  what's in front of you that you're  missing the bigger picture with this  realization I search for a better way to  invest in and support businesses that is  putting on a pair of glasses that would  help me see more clearly and more  comprehensively investing with and  impact lens by impact lens I mean asking  yourself more questions before you make  decisions is this truly the best  decision for the long-term  sustainability of the company can I do  this in a way that does good or reduce  harm for society just imagine if someone  at BP had asked those questions the  impact lens is for everyone not just  impact investors or social entrepreneurs  it doesn't mean making bad decisions to  do good it means making better decisions  to do good and do well your ears better  be perking up because I'm telling you  the impact lens can make you more money  with every business and investment  decision you have to evaluate two things  opportunities and risks the impact lens  can help you see both new opportunities  and new risks you might not be seeing I  want to tell you two stories to show you  what I mean the first story is about  opportunities Jon scherhorn was a former  English squash player and an  entrepreneur looking for new  opportunities as an athlete he noticed  that all gyms in the UK were located in  wealthy metropolitan areas charging high  prices  this made him think a low-cost gym  concept targeting lower-income markets  could do a lot of good and be really  successful so he wrote a business plan  and then he sought out funding but  investor after investor rejected him it  wouldn't work they said if Flor income  markets were so attractive gyms would  already be there then one day he found a  match bridges an impact investment fund  had also concluded that low costumes  would be an attractive opportunity to  deliver financial returns and societal  impact together treehorn and bridges  incubated the gym group a chain of  twenty four seven low-cost gyms targeted  at lower income markets the gym group  was a massive success in 2015 the  company IPO at a valuation of 380  million dollars almost six times the  original investment private equity funds  typically target two to three times  today the gym group has more than 130  germs and 660 thousand members now while  the gym space was already crowded no one  had thought lower income markets for an  attractive opportunity sridhara and  bridges saw this opportunity because  they had their impact lenses on so  whether you want to start run or invest  in businesses how can you use the impact  lens to see opportunities you might not  be seeing the impact lens also helps you  see hidden risks Paul Polman became the  CEO of Unilever in 2009 pullman was a  strong believer in doing well by doing  good with his impact lens on he saw  opportunities to grow in emerging  markets he also saw a risk that no one  was talking about climate change Pullman  thought climate change was the single  biggest threat to Unilever and no  investor had ever asked him a question  about it Pullman declared  in ten years I'm going to double  profitability while cutting  environmental footprint in half becoming  more environmentally sustainable was  going to cut costs and reduce risks he  said people thought he was crazy the  results eight years of positive growth  twice the rate of Unilever competitors  total shareholder return of nearly three  hundred percent in that time period  returns and profits are up but even more  importantly long term risks are down  Unilever is better prepared for climate  change than any of its competitors now  climate change is just one example of a  societal issue threatening corporate  welfare global poverty employees skills  gaps political and social instability  nearsighted businesses will say these  are not my problems but if we put our  impact lenses on we can see that these  issues will hit businesses before they  know it now I'm not saying that every  decision is going to be a win-win but  win-win situations are out there and we  absolutely have to chase every single  one of them john treehorn and Paul  Polman are two pioneers in this movement  slowly others are catching up just last  year the CEO of the C of Blackrock Larry  Fink said in a statement to CEOs to  prosper over time every business must  not only deliver financial performance  but also show how it makes a positive  contribution to society what we need are  more business leaders and institutional  investors with their impact lenses on we  also need personal investors and  consumers demanding for more impact from  businesses we Millennials have to take  that charge we're known as the  generation of FOMO fear of missing out  well guess what we're missing out big  time because we're not using the impact  lens in our business thinking we're also  known as a generation of self  entitlement well I think we are entitled  to a better future where businesses can  be part of the solution and not just the  problem we're a fresh set of lenses can  help us see the world in a better light  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

